HEK: WT and MTRF1 KO
From sequencing reads to occupancy tables and plots
Use this guide to compare WT with MTRF1 KO samples in HEK cells. Commands are reusable examples. Adapt the inputs and settings to your experiment before running them.
Original script/workflow credit: Annika Krüger, recorded as creator or editor of the supplied source documents.
1. Prepare your samples
Condition	Replicate display labels
WT	WT1; WT2; WT3
MTRF1 KO	MTRF1 KO1; MTRF1 KO2; MTRF1 KO3

Use unique file IDs such as HEK_WT1 and HEK_MTRF1_MTRF1A_dKO3. The labels above are template slots: enter the actual replicate metadata for your experiment.
Make a working sample CSV containing the six intended HEK samples above. Set sample_sheet in HEK_settings.json to that CSV and set r_analysis.condition_order to ["WT", "MTRF1 KO"]. Use a separate output_dir for this comparison.
Required sample columns: sample_id, cell_line, condition, replicate, sample_label, fastq_path, vector_folder and offset_file. FASTQ files may keep their original filenames. Python --samples filters processing only; R reads every row for the configured cell line in sample_sheet.
Inputs to supply
Input	What to check
Sequencing reads	FASTQ paths for each sample; the supplied alignment wrapper uses single-end reads.
Reference and annotation	Alignment FASTA, GTF/BED and start-region ROI files must use matching sequence names and coordinates.
Offsets and R inputs	Reviewed offsets for retained read lengths, gene-to-vector TSV mapping and matching analysis sequences.

Keep the scripts and supporting configuration files together. Run commands from that directory. Existing count vectors can enter at the R analysis step after the same input review.

 2. Configure and process reads
Use HEK_settings.json as an editable example. Its current values come from the source workflow; choose values that match your library preparation, reference and quality checks.
Setting	Adapt for your experiment
adapter; read-length ranges	Enter the library adapter and the length range used at each stage.
Reference and output paths	Set reference_fasta, bowtie2_index, annotation_gtf, annotation_bed, ROI paths and output directories.
Offset calibration	For standard plastid P-site calibration at start codons, set offset_roi_key to roi_start and provide the corresponding ROI file. The supplied profiles currently select roi_stop; review this explicitly.
Review flag	Set settings_confirmed to true only after checking the processing settings. Final offsets and mapping_site are reviewed in step 3.

Install tools once
conda env create -f preprocess_environment.yml
conda env create -f plastid_environment.yml
Rscript install_R_packages.R
Requirements: Python 3.9+, Cutadapt, Bowtie2, SAMtools, plastid and R. The environment files are installation examples; record the software versions you use.
Preview and run
PROFILE=HEK
python3 01_process_reads.py --analysis "$PROFILE" --stage align --dry-run
The preview prints commands and does not process data. After checking the paths and settings, run:
conda activate mitoribo-preprocessing
python3 01_process_reads.py --analysis "$PROFILE" --stage index
python3 01_process_reads.py --analysis "$PROFILE" --stage trim
python3 01_process_reads.py --analysis "$PROFILE" --stage align
conda activate mitoribo-plastid
python3 01_process_reads.py --analysis "$PROFILE" --stage psite
Index builds the reference index; trim removes adapters and filters lengths; align produces sorted, indexed BAM files; psite produces candidate offsets and calibration plots. Review the alignment and calibration outputs before continuing.

 3. Review offsets and analyze vectors
Confirm the mapped ribosome position
Standard plastid psite estimates P-site offsets using start-codon windows. Inspect the plots and check whether that method fits your footprint protocol. Supply a reviewed offset for every retained read length and record mapping_site as P, A or custom. This field records the chosen position; it does not convert offsets to another ribosome site.
Set each sample’s offset_file, or the shared offset_file in the JSON settings. Inspect phasing by read length after applying the final offsets, then export counts and vectors.
python3 01_process_reads.py --analysis "$PROFILE" --stage phase
python3 01_process_reads.py --analysis "$PROFILE" --stage counts
python3 01_process_reads.py --analysis "$PROFILE" --stage vectors
Check R coordinates and run
In HEK_transcripts.tsv, check Gene, vector_file, sequence_gene and vector_start_1based. The start is the 1-based vector position corresponding to the first base of the selected analysis sequence. Confirm strand orientation, reading frame and sequence extent, then set r_analysis.coordinates_confirmed to true. The included per-gene FASTA is an R sequence lookup; it is not a replacement for the alignment reference.
Rscript 02_analyze_vectors.R "$PROFILE"
R uses jsonlite, dplyr and ggplot2. For existing vectors, set r_analysis.vector_dir to their root directory and vector_folder to each sample’s folder name; use the matching vector filenames in the TSV.
Outputs and interpretation
Output	Purpose
BAMs, logs and phasing	Review alignment, retained read lengths and offset behavior.
Counts and vectors	Transcript-level counts and per-nucleotide mapped counts.
analysis/ tables and plots	Sample metadata, codon occupancy, nucleotide counts and coordinate/QC summaries; candidate-stop summaries are exploratory.

Codon occupancy is the sum of three adjacent nucleotide counts divided by the total counts in the selected sequence region, multiplied by 100. Condition averages are descriptive summaries; the scripts do not perform differential hypothesis tests.
For another cell line or reference, adapt a settings copy, sample table and gene mapping. WORKFLOW.md gives the custom-config commands and the full input-field reference. Keep your final settings, software versions and repository release or commit with the analysis.
